INSERT INTO feature VALUES('attested-verdict-cache','kernel','The attested kernel verdict cache','kernel-readmission','partial','uncommon','dependent provers','`gandr check` keeps each declaration its kernel checked as a record under its BLAKE3 entry key. The entry key binds the kernel''s build to the declaration''s content key, which reads no build. The content key covers what the declaration reads: its segment with each position it names written by where the segment first names it, the content keys of those positions, the content keys of the types of words ranked below it over each sort its references reach, in rank order, and of the atoms manifest at it, and for a generative declaration a count of the earlier ones with its content ranked below it. Each entry holds a `SourceRank`, its place in its revision''s source order, which a fresh admission sets to its position: the content key of a definition with operation rules adds the rank order of the operations its rules name, its own among them, and that of a holder of a built-in role the content key of each role''s holder ranked first below it. So an edit re-keys only the declarations that read the edited one, and a declaration keeps its key at another position, after an edit before it, and in another order when it has no operation rules. No key names a place in the admission order, and a recall reads the entry key alone. A declaration that names a vacancy or a retired entry has no entry key, so the kernel checks it. Two kernel builds give a declaration one content key and two entry keys, so a new build misses each record once. The records sit under a receipt this installation''s Ed25519 key signs, and a later `check` admits a declaration on a recorded verdict only under a receipt a key its policy trusts signed: its own and each `kernel.trusted` lists. The receipts are values in the storage tier''s store, beside the stratum store''s strata, and a receipt the store refuses costs a replay. The language server and the hub keep their receipts in the same store under the same key and policy, and in the process the ones kept after the store opens. So a recheck in the same process and a later session both recall each declaration that did not change, and the tier lens shows it as `kernel admitted: attested`. The store evicts no receipt, and a process reads each one when it opens the store. With no cache directory, a server keeps its receipts in the process alone, under a key the process mints and trusts alone. `--recheck`, `kernel.policy = "recheck"`, `gandr test` and the corpus gate check every declaration. Owed: the eviction of records that miss twice (#6734).','kernel-term kernel-core core-checker surface-dispatcher surface-driver surface-lsp','crates/kernel-core/tests/verdicts.rs crates/kernel-core/src/environment.rs crates/kernel-term/src/encode.rs crates/core-checker-bridge/src/bridge/tests.rs crates/surface-dispatcher/src/receipts.rs crates/surface-dispatcher/src/verdicts.rs crates/surface-driver/src/verdicts.rs crates/surface-session/tests/counts.rs crates/surface-driver/tests/cli.rs',NULL,'#6734');
